## The basic conditions

**Dirichlet, $s = s_b$ on the boundary.** Linear interpolation
between the two centres gives the face value $\tfrac12(s_i + s_g)$; setting
it to $s_b$ gives
$$
s_g = 2s_b - s_i.
\qquad (18.1)
$$

**Neumann, $\partial_n s = q$ on the boundary.** The face gradient
$(s_g - s_i)/\Delta$ must equal $q$:
$$
s_g = s_i + \Delta\,q.
\qquad (18.2)
$$

**A bare expression.** If the condition is an expression without
`dirichlet()` or `neumann()`, the ghost value is set to that
expression directly. This is the safe way to impose a volume fraction at
an inlet ([section 18.8](#sec:bc-f)).

**Symmetry and antisymmetry.** `symmetry` sets $s_g = s_i$ (a
homogeneous Neumann condition) and `antisymmetry` sets $s_g = -s_i$ (a
homogeneous Dirichlet condition, $s_b = 0$).

> **In the code: `grid/cartesian-common.h` and `embed.h`**
>
> ~~~literatec
> double dirichlet (double expr, ...) { return 2.*expr - s[]; }
> double neumann   (double expr, ...) { return Delta*expr + s[]; }
> static double symmetry     (...) { return s[]; }
> static double antisymmetry (...) { return -s[]; }
> ~~~
>
> `embed.h` redefines `dirichlet()` and `neumann()` so that the
> same names work on the embedded boundary, where they return the boundary
> value or gradient instead of a ghost value ([chapter 19](19_embed)).

## Default conditions

If you set nothing on a side:

- every scalar is `symmetry`: zero normal gradient;

- for a vector, the normal component is `antisymmetry` (zero on the
  boundary) and the tangential components are `symmetry` (zero normal
  gradient), which together are a *free-slip* wall;

- the pressure $p$ gets the Neumann condition of [section 15.6](15_projection#sec:pbc) that
  balances the face acceleration. On a wall face, $u_f^\star = \Delta tf_m a_n$
  (the averaged velocity is zero because of the antisymmetric ghost), and the
  projection removes exactly that, so the face velocity through the wall is
  zero after every step.

A box with no boundary conditions set is therefore a closed box with
free-slip walls.

## Vectors: normal and tangential components

Conditions on a vector are written per component relative to the boundary:
`u.n[left]` is the component normal to the left side ($u_x$),
`u.t[left]` the first tangential component ($u_y$) and
`u.r[left]` the second ($u_z$). A no-slip wall is
`u.n[side] = dirichlet(0); u.t[side] = dirichlet(0)` (and `u.r` in
3D).

## Periodic boundaries

`periodic(right)` links the left and right sides: the ghost cells on
one side are copies of the interior cells on the other side. It must be
called before the grid is built (`init_grid` or `run`). No
condition may be set on a periodic side.

## Homogeneous conditions in the multigrid solver

The multigrid correction $\delta a$ of [chapter 16](16_multigrid) satisfies the
*homogeneous* version of the boundary conditions of $a$: Dirichlet with
$s_b = 0$ ($s_g = -s_i$) and Neumann with $q = 0$ ($s_g = s_i$). The
correct boundary values are already in the current guess, so the correction
must not change them. Basilisk generates the homogeneous version
automatically from each condition you write.

## Making the pressure problem well posed {#sec:wellposed}

The pressure Poisson problem decides whether a set of conditions works.
Integrate [(15.2)](15_projection) over the domain: the left side becomes the boundary
integral of $\alpha\partial_n p$, the right side the net volume flux of
$\mathbf{u}_f^\star$ through the boundary divided by $\Delta t$.

- If every side has a Neumann condition on $p$ (walls, prescribed
  inflow), the net prescribed volume flux must be zero, or the equation has no
  solution and the multigrid solver never converges.

- If at least one side has a Dirichlet condition on $p$, the problem has
  a unique solution and any imbalance between inflow and outflow leaves
  through that side.

This is why an open boundary is always written as a pair: a condition on
the velocity and a compatible condition on the pressure.

| Boundary type | velocity | pressure |
|:---|:---|:---|
| Free-slip wall (default) | $u_n = 0$, $\partial_n u_t = 0$ | Neumann (balances $a_n$) |
| No-slip wall | $u_n = 0$, $u_t = 0$ | Neumann (default) |
| Velocity inlet | $u_n = U(y,t)$, $u_t$ given | `neumann(0)` |
| Pressure outlet | $\partial_n u_n = 0$ | `dirichlet(0)` |
| Periodic | copied | copied |

