and `pf` need the pressure condition.

## The volume fraction at a boundary {#sec:bc-f}

$f$ appears in the interfacial force as $\phi\,(f_i - f_{i-1})/\Delta$
([(14.2)](14_acceleration)). At a boundary face this uses the ghost value of $f$.
A Dirichlet condition on $f$ with a 0/1 target produces, by
(18.1), ghost values $2f_b - f_i$ that can be $-1$ or $2$
wherever the computed interface is not exactly at the target. The jump
$f_g - f_i$ is then up to 2 instead of at most 1, and the gravity potential
multiplies it into a large force on the first interior face. The safe
choices are `neumann(0)` (the default) or a ghost value that is itself
a volume fraction, for example the exact cut-cell fraction of the incoming
interface computed with `plane_volume()`.

